# Fixed 2026-09-30, defect 132's class, lane recovery-b4, beside
# `fixedbugs-132-a-backslash-joins-its-line-to-the-next`. The `\` is one
# message in each of these, where the line below it went as a block no head
# opens, and no fix is certain: a comment after the `\` or on a line between
# would go into the line the join writes, which then takes the statement's
# other join with it (`line_joins`); and where the line joined is refused for
# the join alone, `if a` taking its body into its head or two values meeting
# on one line, the join repairs nothing, and deleting the `\` is as likely
# (`parse/apart`). So `check --apply` leaves the file as it is.

function a_comment_after()
    x = 1 + \  #~ unexpected_character
        2
    print(x)

function a_comment_line_between()
    #~v unexpected_character
    x = 1 + \
        # the second operand
        2
    print(x)

function twice_with_a_comment_between()
    #~v unexpected_character
    x = 1 + \
        #~v unexpected_character
        2 + \
        3
    print(x)

function over_the_body_of_its_head(a: bool)
    #~v unexpected_character
    if a \
        print(1)

function a_value_over_a_value()
    #~v unexpected_character
    x = 1 \
        2
    print(x)
